  "query_preview": "..\nActivity has largely quieted down during the overnight hours\nafter an active afternoon across portions of the forecast area\nWednesday. A moist atmosphere in conjunction with a potent\nsubtropical jet streak and trough axis resulted in pockets of\nheavy rainfall. TUS airport reported 0.65\" rain, a record\nrainfall for the day and 10th highest June rainfall on record.\nA couple of ALERT gages across Pima County reported local totals\nabove 1.50\" rain. The main impacts reported were multiple swift\nwater rescues and downed power poles.\n\nThunderstorm activity is on the decrease today as the weather \npattern subtly shifts. While the 500mb low that brought\nyesterday's thunderstorms remains in place, the subtropical jet\nhas pushed northward. The result is generally less atmospheric\nmoisture across the region, and the best support for\nthunderstorms today along the White Mountains. Otherwise, drier\nweather is forecast for the rest of the region with near normal\ntemperatures. \n\nThe upper low will phase in with the broader longwave trough\nacross the Northwest US by Friday. From this time period through\nthe weekend, broad westerly flow aloft will dominate over the\nregion as high pressure remains to our south. Temperatures will\nremain seasonably hot through this timeframe. The pattern will\namplify early to mid next week with high pressure building on\ntop of us. This will lead to a warming trend across the region.\nCurrent forecasts have widespread Moderate to locally High\nHeatRisk by the middle of next week.",
